                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose: why do these businesses belong together?`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Before you touch the deal pipeline, do the corporate-theory archaeology. Cascade has compounded value for two decades by buying and improving undermanaged industrial makers — a recipe, not a theory. Find the <strong>latent connective tissue</strong>: what does Cascade understand or own that rivals don't? The strongest candidate is <strong>embedded sensing</strong> across flow and water systems. Then name honestly the segment with <em>no coherent reason</em> to be there, and read the real cause of the 22% discount: the market sees no value-adding logic to common ownership. Diagnose first — the bets follow from the diagnosis.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Articulate the corporate theory`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Write Cascade's theory in Zenger's terms: a forward-looking, internally consistent hypothesis combining <strong>foresight</strong> (where industrial markets head), <strong>insight</strong> (Cascade's distinctive assets) and <strong>cross-sight</strong> (which assets are worth more under Cascade). The trap: a statement so broad any rival could claim it — “we create value through operational excellence” — rejects nothing and predicts nothing. <em>Narrow it until it can be wrong.</em> A real theory draws a boundary: it tells you what NOT to own, however large the TAM.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate capital: select the bets that fit`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Run the four bets through your theory under the <strong>~$1,000M ceiling</strong> and the 2.5x leverage limit. HydroSense ($620M) and the Software build ($180M) extend the sensing-to-systems logic; Apex Pumps ($240M) is an accretive but logic-neutral bolt-on; <em>Verdana Robotics ($410M) is the growth trap</em> — large TAM, zero overlap, the only deal exciting enough to satisfy the board fast, and the worst fit. A positive-NPV deal that dilutes coherence destroys corporate value. Make at least one explicit, theory-based rejection — the rejection is what gives the strategy its teeth.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Present at Capital Allocation Day; defend coherence`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present to the board and Meridian Point. The board pushes for the big growth deal; the activist tests whether your theory is real or post-hoc. <strong>Defend the rejection</strong> as proof the theory has teeth, <strong>quantify coherence</strong> (a credible narrow theory closes half the discount — ~$370m of equity value), and <strong>commit to a falsifiable 18-month proof point</strong>. If you can't name a metric and a date that would prove you wrong, the activist calls it storytelling and takes the board seats.`,

