                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_tissue_right:  `<strong>That's the connective tissue.</strong> Embedded sensing across flow and water systems is something Cascade captures end-to-end and a standalone rival can't assemble. That is a candidate theory — now name the misfit and you have a portfolio to defend.`,
                    coach_r1_tissue_generic:`<strong>“We run good businesses well” is a recipe, not a theory.</strong> Every PE fund and every discounted conglomerate says the same. Operational excellence won't tell you what to buy or reject. What does Cascade specifically <em>see or own</em> that rivals don't?`,
                    coach_r1_tissue_none:   `<strong>If there is truly no connective tissue, the activist is right and you should break up.</strong> But you haven't looked hard enough — the embedded sensing across your industrial systems is exactly the latent logic the market can't see yet.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named embedded sensing as the connective tissue AND flagged warehouse robotics as the misfit. That is the lens that will let you reject the exciting-but-incoherent deal later without flinching.`,
                    coach_r1_nofit:         `<strong>“Every segment fits” is denial.</strong> The discount isn't market noise — it's the market's verdict that there's no value-adding logic. Some of what you own genuinely doesn't belong; saying everything fits means the theory rejects nothing.`,
                    coach_r1_ir:            `<strong>This isn't a storytelling problem.</strong> You can't IR your way out of a 22% discount. The market sees no logic because the capital allocation hasn't shown one. Fix the logic, not the narrative.`,
                    coach_r1_size:          `<strong>Size isn't the cause.</strong> Plenty of focused firms your size trade at a premium. The discount is about coherence, not scale — diversified firms trade below the sum of their parts when there's no logic to common ownership.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>You've skipped the diagnosis and reached for the pipeline.</strong> Ranking deals by IRR before you know why Cascade owns these businesses together is exactly the activist's charge. Do the archaeology first.`,
                    coach_r2_narrow:        `<strong>That theory can be wrong — which is why it's useful.</strong> Sensing-to-analytics in flow and water is specific to Cascade and false of your rivals. It tells you what to buy and, crucially, what to reject. That is a corporate theory, not a slogan.`,
                    coach_r2_broad:         `<strong>So does every PE fund and every conglomerate the market discounts.</strong> “Operational excellence and disciplined capital allocation” is true of Cascade and true of everyone else — so it isn't yours. Narrow it until it can be wrong.`,
                    coach_r2_growth:        `<strong>“Pursue the largest TAMs” is the activist's nightmare, not a theory.</strong> Chasing big markets with no shared logic is precisely the incoherent diversification driving the discount. A theory constrains the search; it doesn't bless every large opportunity.`,
                    coach_r2_breakup:       `<strong>Breaking up concedes you can't create cross-segment value.</strong> You'd hand the activist the win and destroy the sensing-and-software upside you just identified. A theory is a constructive argument for staying together — make it, or admit honestly there is none.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>All three components are doing work.</strong> Foresight, insight and cross-sight in balance — with cross-sight carrying its weight — is what makes the theory specific to Cascade. Cross-sight is the binding piece: which assets are worth more under you.`,
                    coach_r2_lopsided:      `<strong>You've starved one of Zenger's components.</strong> A theory built on foresight alone (or insight alone) isn't internally consistent. Cross-sight — which complementary assets are worth more under Cascade — is what turns a view into a portfolio logic.`,
                    coach_r2_lowmargin:     `<strong>“Keep everything profitable” isn't a boundary.</strong> A standalone-attractive business can still destroy corporate value by diluting the theory. The boundary has to be about <em>fit</em>, not just margin.`,
                    coach_r2_nobound:       `<strong>A theory that rejects nothing predicts nothing.</strong> “Nothing is off-limits” is how you end up as a grab-bag. If a good business is always worth owning, you have no theory — you have a pipeline.`,
                    coach_r3_verdana:       `<strong>You just funded the $410m robotics deal that fits nothing you own.</strong> You relieved this quarter's growth pressure and proved Meridian's point: Cascade buys what's exciting, not what's coherent. The discount widens, not narrows. Was the growth worth confirming the diagnosis the activist is using to take your board seats?`,
                    coach_r3_overcap:       `<strong>You're over the ~$1.0bn ceiling.</strong> A strategy that funds everything is no strategy — and it pushes leverage toward the 3.0x ceiling with no cushion for a downturn. Which bet does your theory tell you to reject?`,
                    coach_r3_overcap_verdana:`<strong>Over the ceiling AND funding the growth trap.</strong> Verdana blew the budget and the logic at once — leverage near 3.0x with no cushion, and the most incoherent deal in the portfolio. This is exactly the grab-bag the activist is attacking.`,
                    coach_r3_reject_theory: `<strong>That rejection is the test of your theory.</strong> Rejecting Verdana on the logic — not the financials — is what gives the strategy teeth. A disciplined “no” to an attractive deal is worth more to the discount than any single approval.`,
                    coach_r3_reject_contradiction:`<strong>You said reject on the theory but funded Verdana anyway.</strong> A rejection rationale you don't act on is post-hoc storytelling. If the theory rejects warehouse robotics, the capital has to reject it too.`,
                    coach_r3_reject_financials:`<strong>Rejecting on financials alone misses the point.</strong> The hardest bet to reject — Verdana — has a positive NPV. The reason to reject it is that it dilutes the theory and the coherence the market is pricing, not that its IRR is lowest.`,
                    coach_r3_reject_none:   `<strong>A portfolio with no rejection is a pipeline, not a strategy.</strong> If every bet that clears the hurdle gets funded, your theory governs nothing. The rejections are what prove the theory has teeth.`,
                    coach_r3_growthfirst:   `<strong>Leading with the biggest growth deal reads as opportunism.</strong> Sequencing the connective-tissue bets first — Software and HydroSense — signals to the market that capital follows the theory, not the board's impatience.`,
                    coach_r4_discipline:    `<strong>That's a board-ready defence.</strong> You framed the rejection as proof the theory has teeth and showed coherence — not opportunism — is what closes the discount. That re-rates the stock and defends the board seats.`,
                    coach_r4_concede:       `<strong>You conceded and added Verdana under pressure.</strong> The growth number ticks up this quarter, but you've just confirmed Meridian's thesis live in the boardroom — Cascade buys what's exciting, not what's coherent. The discount widens and the activist wins the argument.`,
                    coach_r4_breakup:       `<strong>Proposing a break-up to dodge the fight hands the activist the win.</strong> You destroy the cross-segment value you spent three rounds identifying. A corporate theory is an argument for staying together — defend it with capital or admit there was none.`,
                    coach_r4_eps:           `<strong>Pointing the board to next-quarter EPS misses the prize.</strong> The big value is the discount: half of 22% is ~$370m of equity value. Near-term accretion from bolt-ons won't re-rate the stock — coherence will.`,
                    coach_r4_tam:           `<strong>Selling them on combined TAM is the grab-bag pitch.</strong> “Look how big the opportunity is” is exactly the opportunism the activist is attacking. Quantify coherence — discount closure — not the size of everything you could buy.`,
                    coach_r4_proof:         `<strong>A named metric and a date — that's a falsifiable theory.</strong> Cross-segment analytics revenue and attach-rate by a set point lets the board hold you to it and tells the activist this is a real thesis, not storytelling.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>“A higher-quality portfolio over time” is not falsifiable.</strong> If you can't name a metric and a date that would prove you wrong, the board can't hold you to it and Meridian calls it post-hoc narrative. Commit to a specific 18-month test.`,
                    coach_r4_noproof:       `<strong>“The strategy speaks for itself” is what every failed theory says.</strong> Without a falsifiable proof point the activist is right to be sceptical. Name the result in 18 months that would tell you the sensing-to-software logic is wrong.`,

